  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>If we heat-up an object, then, the&nbsp;energy is used to either change the temperature of the body or to change the state of the body.<br />If it&nbsp;changes the temperature, then,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Q</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>m</mi><mi>S</mi><mo>&#8710;</mo><mi>T</mi></math><br />where,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Q</mi></math>&nbsp;is the heat given,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>m</mi></math>&nbsp;is the mass and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8710;</mo><mi>T</mi></math>&nbsp;is the change in temperature.</p><p>We know that a moving object has&nbsp;an energy in the form of kinetic energy, i.e.,<br /><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>K</mi><mi>E</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mi>m</mi><msup><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math></p><p>When that moving object strikes another object,&nbsp;it&nbsp;loses some energy which&nbsp;changes into the heat&nbsp;energy and this heat energy increases the temperature of the object.<br />Given&nbsp;a hammer of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>kg</mi></math>&nbsp;is moving with&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>60</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math>.<br />Kinetic energy&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mi>m</mi><msup><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>(</mo><mn>60</mn><msup><mo>)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>2700</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J</mi></math><br />One-fourth of kinetic energy changes into heat energy.<br /><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8658;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mo>(</mo><mi>K</mi><mi>E</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>=</mo></math>&nbsp;Heat energy<br /><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8658;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mo>(</mo><mi>K</mi><mi>E</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>(</mo><mo>&#8710;</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>)</mo></math><br /><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8658;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mo>(</mo><mn>2700</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>(</mo><mn>100</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>42</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>(</mo><mo>&#8710;</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>)</mo></math><br /><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8658;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#8710;</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>16</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>07</mn><mo>&#176;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></math></p>",
